@meta
id: 26-10
title: Read a command's output into the file
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: read-shell
par: 27
minutes: 5
vscode: run it in the terminal panel, select the output, copy, paste
open: inventory.md

@brief
This inventory document has a heading with nothing under it. Under
`## Domain files` belongs the list of files in `src/domain`, one per
line, as `ls` prints it.

A filter replaces lines. This is the other half: `:r !cmd` runs a command
and *inserts* its output, changing nothing that was already there. The
output lands after the line you address, exactly like `:r file` reads a
file in.

Leave everything under `## Notes` alone, and do not open the source files.

@keys
| `:r !cmd` | insert the command's output after the current line |
| `:6r !cmd` | insert it after line 6 |
| `:0r !cmd` | insert it above the first line |
| `:$r !cmd` | insert it at the end of the file |
| `:r file` | the same command without the `!` — reads a file, not a command |

@hints
You want the output to land directly under the heading, so address the
heading's line and read after it.
--
`:r` takes an address in front of it like every other ex command, and a
`!` after it turns the argument from a file name into a shell command.
--
`:6r !ls src/domain` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :6r !ls src/domain<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

`:r !` is the one people go looking for when they have got used to
copying out of a terminal. Anything a command can print can be poured
straight into the buffer: `:r !git log --oneline -10` to draft a release
note, `:r !ls -1 migrations | tail -5` when you need the last few
migration names spelled right, `:r !date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` to stamp
a changelog entry, `:r !python3 -c 'print(2**32)'` when you want a number
you cannot be bothered to work out.

The address is the whole trick. `:r !` on its own reads after the cursor
line. `:0r !cmd` is the special one: line 0 means "before line 1", so it
is how a generated header, a licence block or a table of contents gets to
the top of a file without you moving there first. `:$r !cmd` appends at
the bottom.

One gotcha that will bite you exactly once: in any `:!` command, an
unescaped `%` is replaced by the name of the current file and `#` by the
alternate file. Usually that is a gift — `:r !wc -l %` counts this very
file. When you actually want a literal percent sign, as in a `date +%d`
format or an `awk` modulo, escape it: `\%`.

@start inventory.md
# Order service inventory

Kept by hand, which is why it is always out of date. The list under the
heading below should be whatever is in src/domain today.

## Domain files

## Notes

The payment gateway client lives in another repo.

@expected inventory.md
# Order service inventory

Kept by hand, which is why it is always out of date. The list under the
heading below should be whatever is in src/domain today.

## Domain files
customer.entity.ts
customer.provider.ts
order.entity.ts
order.provider.ts
order.repository.ts

## Notes

The payment gateway client lives in another repo.

@start src/domain/customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
};

@start src/domain/customer.provider.ts
export class CustomerProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    return this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  total: number;
};

@start src/domain/order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'CANCELLED' } });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<Order> {
  protected model = 'order';
}
